On 07/18/12 19:35, commodorejohn wrote:
> I've recently acquired a MicroPDP-11/23. It's a simple setup, and
> there's only one memory module in the system, a 256KB (half-populated)
> MSV11 card. I was wondering, since this card can also be had in a
> 512KB fully-populated configuration, and since compatible 4164 ICs can
> be had cheaply, whether it would be possible to hand-upgrade this card
> to the full 512KB. Soldering in extra chips is easy enough, but the
> one thing I'm not sure of is that, from the manual, it looks like
> address decoding is done by a couple of PROMs. If I have to replace
> those, is there any documentation out there on what kind of PROMs can
> be used for the purpose, and what data should go into them?

For ease of manufacturing, they may use the same prom on both versions,
but there may also be hard coded jumpers (zero ohm resistors, which are
a length of wire in a resistor body) to enable the added memory. You
really need the print set, with schematics  for both, to see what
the differences are and if there are any prom part number differences
between the two. Otherwise, just try it :-)...

256K will be more than enough to run rt11 and probably others as well...

Regards,

Chris



