Another possibility: Someone moved cards around between machines,
not realizing the clash. When they (or someone else) couldn't get
it to run, they scrapped it.

For the MXV21:

  "The controller is shiped with the DEC standard device address
   and vector assignments preset to 17710 and 264 (octal) respectively.
   Any change in these assignments would necessitate a change in
   system software. However, an alternate address and vector assignment
   is selectable and is defined as 177174 and 270, repectively."

  "To select the alternate address/vector, first cut the foil between
   W15 and W16. Jumper W16 to W17 and jumper W33 to W34."

also

  "The controller board incorporates a transparent bootstrap.
   The bootstrap is initiated whenever the program reads from 173000,
   homing both drives to track 0. Next, track 1 sector 1 of unit
   zero is read and diskette density is determined. If the diskette
   is single density, sectors 1, 3, 5, and 7  are loaded into 
   memory starting at location zero."

  "Controllers are shipped with this feature disabled."

-- glen



